VALUES ('TSLA','bear_case','editorial_analysis','Q2 automotive gross margin was 16.9%, below 17.2% a year earlier, while regulatory-credit revenue fell 67% to $146 million; volume alone does not guarantee stronger unit economics. First-half property-and-equipment purchases more than doubled to $8.282 billion, and management expects 2026 capex above $25 billion before Robotaxi, Cybercab, AI compute, and Optimus reach mature returns. Energy Q2 revenue increased to $3.139 billion, but segment gross profit fell to $640 million from $846 million and gross margin fell to 20.4% from 30.3%. Autonomy, mobility, and robotics face technical, safety, liability, insurance, regulatory, service, and utilization risks; management forecasts and demonstrations are not substitutes for audited segment economics.','Preserves the skeptical interpretation as a sourced, reviewable claim.','Tesla Second-Quarter 2026 Form 10-Q','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000162828026049270/tsla-20260630.htm','The filing reports Q2 and first-half revenue, segment gross profit, net income, cash flow, capital expenditures, liquidity, Robotaxi and AI investment, regulatory credits, legal matters, related-party transactions, and risk disclosures.','2026-07-22','published','Founding editorial research',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
